    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "Found by my agent, but easy to understand. I think it's worth fixing (but I'm not sure yet what's the good fix)\n\n----\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nA `prange` that holds the GIL hangs on GIL builds of Python when compiled with clang, as soon as the loop has a `lastprivate` variable. In practice that's every such loop, since the target always gets one. GCC and free-threaded builds are fine.\n\nClang adds a barrier at the end of a worksharing loop with `lastprivate`, even with `nowait`. That barrier runs right after the last iteration, so before the generated code reaches `PyEval_SaveThread()`. The thread that finishes first waits at this barrier with the GIL held, and the other threads wait for the GIL to run their first iteration.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\n# cython: language_level=3, freethreading_compatible=True\nfrom cython.parallel import prange\n\ndef run(int n=1000):\n    cdef int i\n    cdef int total = 0\n    for i in prange(n, num_threads=4):\n        total += 1\n    return total\n```\n\nWith conda-forge's `clang` and `llvm-openmp`:\n\n```\nCC=clang CFLAGS=\"-fopenmp -I$CONDA_PREFIX/include\" \\\n    LDFLAGS=\"-fopenmp -L$CONDA_PREFIX/lib -Wl,-rpath,$CONDA_PREFIX/lib\" cythonize -i repro.pyx\ntimeout 15 python -c \"import repro; print(repro.run())\"\n```\n\n| compiler | Python | Cython | 5 runs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| clang 21.1 | 3.13 | 3.3.1 | 5 hangs |\n| clang 14.0 | 3.13 | 3.3.1 | 5 hangs |\n| clang 21.1 | 3.13 | master (7b3aab363) | 5 hangs |\n| clang 21.1 | 3.14 | 3.3.1 | 5 hangs |\n| clang 21.1 | 3.13 free-threaded | 3.3.1 | returns 1000 |\n| GCC 15.3 | 3.13 | 3.3.1 | returns 1000 |\n\nBacktrace with clang 21 and Python 3.13, trimmed. The main thread holds the GIL in the barrier, the 3 others are in the loop body:\n\n```\nThread 4, 3, 2:\n#6  take_gil (tstate=...) at Python/ceval_gil.c:341\n#7  _PyEval_AcquireLock (tstate=...) at Python/ceval_gil.c\n#8  _PyThreadState_Attach (tstate=...) at Python/pystate.c:2100\n#9  PyEval_RestoreThread (tstate=...) at Python/ceval_gil.c:663\n#10 __pyx_pf_5repro_run.omp_outlined_debug__ (...) at repro.c:2737   <- first statement of the loop body\n\nThread 1:\n#5  __kmp_suspend_64<false, true>(int, kmp_flag_64<false, true>*) ()\n#6  __kmp_wait_template<kmp_flag_64<false, true>, false, false, true>(...) ()\n#7  __kmp_hyper_barrier_gather(...) ()\n#8  __kmp_barrier_template<false>(...) ()\n#9  __kmpc_barrier () from libomp.so\n#10 __pyx_pf_5repro_run.omp_outlined_debug__ (...) at repro.c:2731   <- the \"omp for\" pragma\n```\n\nClang reports all the barriers of the loop on the pragma line, so I checked the disassembly: the return address of thread 1 is right after the `__kmpc_barrier` call that follows `__kmpc_for_static_fini`, just before `PyEval_SaveThread`.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n`run()` returns 1000, as it does with GCC.\n\n### OS\n\nLinux (Ubuntu 24.04)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.13 and 3.14, GIL builds\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.1 and master\n\n\n",
