        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> My current thought is to do better about identifying variables that are never used after the loop (which has to be a decent number of loop targets). That would let a good chunk of code work in clang (and the remainder could get an informative #error). I'll do this in the near future unless you tell me you have a better plan.\n\nI have a vibe-coded PR on my fork that is exactly doing that (https://github.com/cakedev0/cython/pull/4). It removes the vast majority of real-world cases and reduces tremendously the number of barriers in the generated code for scikit-learn. I'm in the process of gaining a good enough understanding of it before opening it upstream.\n\nI can continue that (I should be able to open it today or tomorrow), or if you want to take it over I'm happy with it (in which case I'll give a hand at reviewing the PR).\n\nFYI, barrier counts in scikit-learn:\n\n| Cython                       | GCC | clang 21  |\n| ---------------------------- | --- | --------- |\n| 3.3.1                        | 399 | 798       |\n| #8121                | 465 | 798       |\n| #8121 (explicit barrier version)  | 465 | 866 |\n| #8121 + https://github.com/cakedev0/cython/pull/4      | 67  | 69        |\n| #8121 (explicit barrier) + https://github.com/cakedev0/cython/pull/4 | 67  | 70  |\n",
